5. Symmetries: K -> -K (rotation), (K, om) -> (-K, -om) (conjugation), swap map; degeneracies
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PASS  R = T1 T2 is unitary, commutes with T0, T4, bet, J and anticommutes with T1: R maps a solution at (K, om) to one at (-K, om) with the same wall condition B(vp)
  PASS  R anticommutes with w3: it maps irrep w3 = -i onto w3 = +i
  PASS  complex conjugation: (T0 (m - iK T1 + i om T4))^* = T0 (m + iK T1 - i om T4): it maps (K, om) -> (-K, -om) (checked at K = 1/3, om = 2/7, m = 5/11 and symbolically with real K, om, m)
  PASS  conjugation maps B(vp) to B(Pi - vp) (bet^* = -bet): among the family it preserves exactly the MIT conditions vp = Pi/2, 3Pi/2
  PASS  hence, with MIT walls, the 16-component spectrum at K is symmetric: S(K) = S(-K) = -S(K)
  PASS  swap map: (f, g) solves the w3=-i ODE at (K, om) iff (g, f) solves it at (-K, -om); the condition f cos th + g sin th = 0 becomes th -> Pi/2 - th
  PASS  MIT conditions (th = +-Pi/4) are fixed by th -> Pi/2 - th (mod Pi)
    CONSEQUENCE (MIT walls):  S_16(K) = A(K) (+) (-A(K)),  A(K) = spectrum of the w3=-i ODE with f(0) = eps g(0);
    the w3 = -i levels occur in 4 blocks (J = +1 twice, J = -1 twice), the w3 = +i levels (= -A(K)) in the other 4.
    A positive level om of S_16 is therefore 4-fold per transverse momentum vector, 8-fold only when om and -om both lie in A(K).
    For the general family (uniform th in all blocks):  S_16(K) = A_th(K) (+) (-A_(Pi/2-th)(K)),  each 4-fold.

